# Multi-Cohort Intracranial Aneurysm Rupture-Risk Prediction

Final-year research project (Rehman Medical Institute) — a rigorous,
reproducible dry-lab pipeline predicting intracranial aneurysm (IA) rupture
status from bulk transcriptomics, validated across independent cohorts and
a blood-based cohort, with single-cell deconvolution and drug-repurposing
follow-up.

**Supervisor:** Dr. Syed Sarmad Bukhari, Rehman Medical Institute (RMI)

## Summary of approach

1. WGCNA on a discovery RNA-seq cohort (GSE122897, n=58 after QC) to find
   rupture-associated gene modules
2. LASSO feature selection + nested cross-validation (with proper
   fold-internal feature selection to avoid leakage) to pick a classifier
   and a minimal gene panel
3. A permutation test to establish a null baseline for the discovery-cohort
   estimate
4. Independent validation of the frozen panel + classifier on 3 separate
   microarray cohorts and 1 peripheral-blood cohort
5. scRNA-seq-based deconvolution to estimate which vascular/immune cell
   types the panel genes are enriched in
6. Drug-repurposing candidate search against the panel (L1000CDS2, DGIdb)

## Results

| Stage | Result |
|---|---|
| Discovery nested-CV AUC | 0.708 (95% CI 0.434–0.942); permutation p = 0.213 (not significant alone — see Limitations) |
| Final gene panel (Random Forest) | BCAM, EXD3, FXYD1, PLCL1, GPR17, PDZD4, INPP4B |
| GSE13353 validation AUC | 0.928 (0.756–1.000) |
| GSE15629 validation AUC | 0.817 (0.489–0.977) |
| GSE54083 validation AUC | 0.621 (0.463–0.873) |
| GSE36791 blood validation AUC | 0.703 (0.548–0.858) |
| scRNA gene–celltype associations (FDR<0.05) | 22 / 35 |

**Honest framing:** the discovery-cohort nested-CV estimate alone is not
statistically distinguishable from chance (permutation p = 0.213), which is
expected given n=58. However, the same frozen panel and classifier show
consistently positive AUCs when independently applied to four separate
cohorts, including blood — this combination (weak discovery-cohort
significance, consistent independent replication) is reported together
rather than cherry-picking the more favorable number.

